(3247666, 449101, 69186, '<td valign="top">From the critique club<br/>Light; highlights blown out on the left, witch would be okay if the background was cleaner and more light on her face.<br/>You could clean the background up with Photoshop since this was an advanced editing challenge. <br/>The pose seems kind of awkward when you crop a part of her head of. <br/>The eyes seem out of focus, it suits it better to have them in focus. And she has very pretty eyes.<br/>Nice PP on the eyes and the colors are lovely.<br/>The composition would be quite nice with the whole head in the picture. <br/>If you have any questions about the critique feel free to contact me through the PM system.<br/></td>', 'From the critique clubLight; highlights blown out on the left, witch would be okay if the background was cleaner and more light on her face.You could clean the background up with Photoshop since this was an advanced editing challenge. The pose seems kind of awkward when you crop a part of her head of. The eyes seem out of focus, it suits it better to have them in focus. And she has very pretty eyes.Nice PP on the eyes and the colors are lovely.The composition would be quite nice with the whole head in the picture. If you have any questions about the critique feel free to contact me through the PM system.', '2007-01-17 15:38:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247772, 449331, 63398, '<td valign="top">I remember commenting on this during the challenge, so I thought I''d be able to dodge this one.....apparently not!<br/> <br/>This is a good solid entry, a little humor and self-deprecation evoked by the title, terrific light, setting, crispness of detail and the right DOF IMHO.</td>', 'I remember commenting on this during the challenge, so I thought I''d be able to dodge this one.....apparently not! This is a good solid entry, a little humor and self-deprecation evoked by the title, terrific light, setting, crispness of detail and the right DOF IMHO.', '2007-01-17 16:00:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247808, 448875, 63398, '<td valign="top">Oh......I''m an idiot!!! I wondered what this was about......you didn''t manage to convey to me that this was a quilt in progress, that said, I''m pretty dumb when it comes to a needle and thread. Decent picture, although it doesn''t really speak to me. That would be because the subject material made me tune it out after I missed the title. I would have liked to see the basket/bin in the upper right hand area of the picture sharpened, but that''s my opinion. Now that I understand, challenge met.</td>', 'Oh......I''m an idiot!!! I wondered what this was about......you didn''t manage to convey to me that this was a quilt in progress, that said, I''m pretty dumb when it comes to a needle and thread. Decent picture, although it doesn''t really speak to me. That would be because the subject material made me tune it out after I missed the title. I would have liked to see the basket/bin in the upper right hand area of the picture sharpened, but that''s my opinion. Now that I understand, challenge met.', '2007-01-17 16:06:49.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247857, 448612, 63398, '<td valign="top">Okay....I gotta suck it up on this one and tell you I gave it a 4 because with this title, it would have made more sense to have the little girl by herself. I think it''s a very good pic......the wall helps make this a small, but powerful look that would have really worked for me had Mom not been in the pic. Mom''s taking that quiet moment with her daughter, so I have to say that it misses the mark for me as a Procrastination entry. I have a wonderful twelve year old myself and I grab these moments whenever I can.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2007-01-17 16:20:45.</i></td>', 'Okay....I gotta suck it up on this one and tell you I gave it a 4 because with this title, it would have made more sense to have the little girl by herself. I think it''s a very good pic......the wall helps make this a small, but powerful look that would have really worked for me had Mom not been in the pic. Mom''s taking that quiet moment with her daughter, so I have to say that it misses the mark for me as a Procrastination entry. I have a wonderful twelve year old myself and I grab these moments whenever I can.', '2007-01-17 16:19:26.000000', '2007-01-17 16:20:45.000000', 0),
(3247877, 449150, 63398, '<td valign="top">Now that I know that you''re someone I know and love.......STOP NOW.<br/> <br/>That''s it, stop now.....you''ll feel better in three weeks and it keeps getting better. Your clothes won''t stink, you''ll be able to breathe and taste food.....for me, my voice changed immensely, and I sleep much better. <br/> <br/>The picture''s fine!....8&gt;)</td>', 'Now that I know that you''re someone I know and love.......STOP NOW. That''s it, stop now.....you''ll feel better in three weeks and it keeps getting better. Your clothes won''t stink, you''ll be able to breathe and taste food.....for me, my voice changed immensely, and I sleep much better.  The picture''s fine!....8>)', '2007-01-17 16:25:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(3247919, 448341, 63398, '<td valign="top">I passed on voting on this when it came up ''cause I didn''t even want to THINK about that lovely hair being cut. I''m a child of the hippie generation and soft, long, brown hair should NEVER be cut. It does NOT look like something out of a slasher movie except that the scissors so disconcert me by their intent they make me not like the image. I''m sorry.<br/> <br/>Technically it''s a fine offering, but I hate those scissors!</td>', 'I passed on voting on this when it came up ''cause I didn''t even want to THINK about that lovely hair being cut. I''m a child of the hippie generation and soft, long, brown hair should NEVER be cut. It does NOT look like something out of a slasher movie except that the scissors so disconcert me by their intent they make me not like the image. I''m sorry. Technically it''s a fine offering, but I hate those scissors!', '2007-01-17 16:31:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(3248369, 448654, 42567, '<td valign="top">Fun shot!  Congratulations on the top 20!  Keep''em comming</td>', 'Fun shot!  Congratulations on the top 20!  Keep''em comming', '2007-01-17 18:05:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(3248682, 449136, 62318, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Initial Impressions: Ouch, that kind of hurts the eyes. I understand the high-key but this seems overly strong. Nice composition. Blue Rendition is nice. Fits the challenge nicely.<br/> <br/>Composition: The framing of this subject is terrific, though I think a little bit gets lost in the high-key element. The leading lines in the mountain flows nicely through the frame. I also like the "empty" areas juxataposed against the "busy" areas. It helps with the eye movement. Overal placement is great.<br/> <br/>Technicals: Focus seems fairly good, though alot of this gets lost in the highlights. I like the over tone of this photo, not sure if this was a white balance mistake or purposely done but the blue just works. Sometimes it is hard to tell if this photography was an accident or purposeful as far as color and exposure.<br/> <br/>Possible Suggestions: I think there is a bit too much post going on here. While I like the placement of the individual, the person looks overly sharp and it appears to have been done in post. I don''t mind high key, but this one might have been overly done, actually I am almost tempted to see what this picture would have looked like with normal exposure keeping the same "blue" tint. The clouds looked like they would have been pretty dynamic if not blown out. Maybe a bit more verbose title. <br/> <br/>Overall: Wonderful composition and it helps sell the image. I''m not sure of the artistic direction though, I like the blue tint but not the high-key.<br/></td>', '*Critique Club* Initial Impressions: Ouch, that kind of hurts the eyes. I understand the high-key but this seems overly strong. Nice composition. Blue Rendition is nice. Fits the challenge nicely. Composition: The framing of this subject is terrific, though I think a little bit gets lost in the high-key element. The leading lines in the mountain flows nicely through the frame. I also like the "empty" areas juxataposed against the "busy" areas. It helps with the eye movement. Overal placement is great. Technicals: Focus seems fairly good, though alot of this gets lost in the highlights. I like the over tone of this photo, not sure if this was a white balance mistake or purposely done but the blue just works. Sometimes it is hard to tell if this photography was an accident or purposeful as far as color and exposure. Possible Suggestions: I think there is a bit too much post going on here. While I like the placement of the individual, the person looks overly sharp and it appears to have been done in post. I don''t mind high key, but this one might have been overly done, actually I am almost tempted to see what this picture would have looked like with normal exposure keeping the same "blue" tint. The clouds looked like they would have been pretty dynamic if not blown out. Maybe a bit more verbose title.  Overall: Wonderful composition and it helps sell the image. I''m not sure of the artistic direction though, I like the blue tint but not the high-key.', '2007-01-17 19:13:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(3248745, 446171, 62318, '<td valign="top">*Critique Club*<br/> <br/>Initial Impressions: Fits the challenge well, still perhaps a bit too busy even if trying to convey a message of being disorganized.<br/> <br/>Composition: You have your monitor and paper in the rule of thirds area which stabilizes the image. However, there is really very little eye movement, mostly just at the top of the frame. I do like the slight bend in the desk, conveying the weight of everytihg. However, in the attempt to make things messy you ended up with a very busy image that really isn''t connected. I think the composition is the weak point of the image.<br/> <br/>Technicals: Now we are talking. Most everything is pretty spot on. Sharpness is great. Everything is level. Exposure is good, with just a few blown highlights, but nothing to really work about. White balance and colors are near perfect.<br/> <br/>Possible Ideas: Perhaps cleaning up a little bit in some places and moving the mess in areas so that your eye moves around the frame. Remove the clutter that doesn''t help the picture as with the speaker. Think leading lines with trash! Maybe a bit more depth of field, though that is more of a minor issue. <br/> <br/>Overall: It is a terrific idea for the challenge subject. All the technicals are pretty good just wish the placement of items helped the photo a bit. Nicely done.</td>', '*Critique Club* Initial Impressions: Fits the challenge well, still perhaps a bit too busy even if trying to convey a message of being disorganized. Composition: You have your monitor and paper in the rule of thirds area which stabilizes the image. However, there is really very little eye movement, mostly just at the top of the frame. I do like the slight bend in the desk, conveying the weight of everytihg. However, in the attempt to make things messy you ended up with a very busy image that really isn''t connected. I think the composition is the weak point of the image. Technicals: Now we are talking. Most everything is pretty spot on. Sharpness is great. Everything is level. Exposure is good, with just a few blown highlights, but nothing to really work about. White balance and colors are near perfect. Possible Ideas: Perhaps cleaning up a little bit in some places and moving the mess in areas so that your eye moves around the frame. Remove the clutter that doesn''t help the picture as with the speaker. Think leading lines with trash! Maybe a bit more depth of field, though that is more of a minor issue.  Overall: It is a terrific idea for the challenge subject. All the technicals are pretty good just wish the placement of items helped the photo a bit. Nicely done.', '2007-01-17 19:29:26.000000', NULL, 0),
(3248809, 449253, 16197, '<td valign="top"><b>From the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>How are you doing today?  I am going to touch on a few items with your image.<br/> <br/><b>Colour</b>;<br/>This image has mostly grays, with a peek of rust colour on the chpped edges of the wieght.  There is also a touch of blue in the light which makes me think this was done with natural lighting.<br/> <br/><b>DOF/Focus</b>;<br/>I think the DOF is find for this image.  However, the focus to me seems a bit soft.  Personaly I would like to see the focus a bit sharper, and that can be done by adding a touch more contrast between the lights and darks of the image.  Or with Unsharp Mask tools.<br/> <br/><b>Post</b>;<br/>This image does look straight from the camera.  I think it would have added this image to give it a couple tweeks in an editor program.  A bit adjustments with black levels and contrasts mostly.  Also, this might have worked well as a Black/White image.  You might try that and adjust the levels a bit to get a really dynamic POP! out of the numbers.<br/> <br/><b>Off Items</b>;<br/>The image does seem a bit flat, and also a bit soft in focus. fixes are like I said above.<br/> <br/><b>Other</b>;<br/>I do think this is a good idea, and I like this composition.  If you have any questions about my comments feel free to message me.<br/> <br/>Nice Job<br/> <br/>Andrew ''littlegett''</td>', 'From the Critique Club  How are you doing today?  I am going to touch on a few items with your image. Colour;This image has mostly grays, with a peek of rust colour on the chpped edges of the wieght.  There is also a touch of blue in the light which makes me think this was done with natural lighting. DOF/Focus;I think the DOF is find for this image.  However, the focus to me seems a bit soft.  Personaly I would like to see the focus a bit sharper, and that can be done by adding a touch more contrast between the lights and darks of the image.  Or with Unsharp Mask tools. Post;This image does look straight from the camera.  I think it would have added this image to give it a couple tweeks in an editor program.  A bit adjustments with black levels and contrasts mostly.  Also, this might have worked well as a Black/White image.  You might try that and adjust the levels a bit to get a really dynamic POP! out of the numbers. Off Items;The image does seem a bit flat, and also a bit soft in focus. fixes are like I said above. Other;I do think this is a good idea, and I like this composition.  If you have any questions about my comments feel free to message me. Nice Job Andrew ''littlegett''', '2007-01-17 19:53:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(3249749, 446427, 811, '<td valign="top">CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUE<br/>by karmat<br/> <br/>I get what you were trying to do here.  As one who has several unfinished projects sitting around, my first impression is, "this is a vehicle he/she has been planning on restoring, and hasn''t gotten around to yet."  In that respect, you met the challenge head on, and I think it was a pretty good idea.<br/> <br/>Compositionally, I think it could have been made stronger by getting closer to the Jeep, and letting it fill the frame more rather than allowing the garage "stuff" to show as well.  The angle that you are shooting at is okay, but maybe if you moved more to the left, and showed more of the vehicle, it would give a broader sense of what you were trying to convey.<br/> <br/>Technically, it is a bit difficult to see.  My monitor is very bright, and I can see the details in the hood that is up, but at work earlier, all I could see was the front panel where the headlights are.  Maybe a slight curves or levels adjustment, or the equivalent in your postprocessing program would help.  Or, use a tripod and go for a slower shutter speed (incidentally, it looks like you got the shutter speed 1/100 and aperture 2.8 values reversed in your data field). <br/> <br/>The reddish color is also a bit disconcerting, especially when taken with regard to the colorless surrounding.  If that is the natural color, not a lot to do with it, but if not, maybe desaturating it, or toning it down a bit to the more natural colors.<br/> <br/>Finally, the size is a bit small, and while it is not a huge issue, being slightly larger would have helped, I think, because people could have seen some of the details better.  There is a tutorial under Learn&gt;Tutorials called (something to the effect of) resizing for dpc.  If you have not looked at it, and if you are continuing to have to use smaller dimensions to get under 150, it might be worth reading.  <br/> <br/>If you have any questions, comments, or if I need to further clarify anything, please let me know.<br/> <br/>karmat</td>', 'CRITIQUE CLUB CRITIQUEby karmat I get what you were trying to do here.  As one who has several unfinished projects sitting around, my first impression is, "this is a vehicle he/she has been planning on restoring, and hasn''t gotten around to yet."  In that respect, you met the challenge head on, and I think it was a pretty good idea. Compositionally, I think it could have been made stronger by getting closer to the Jeep, and letting it fill the frame more rather than allowing the garage "stuff" to show as well.  The angle that you are shooting at is okay, but maybe if you moved more to the left, and showed more of the vehicle, it would give a broader sense of what you were trying to convey. Technically, it is a bit difficult to see.  My monitor is very bright, and I can see the details in the hood that is up, but at work earlier, all I could see was the front panel where the headlights are.  Maybe a slight curves or levels adjustment, or the equivalent in your postprocessing program would help.  Or, use a tripod and go for a slower shutter speed (incidentally, it looks like you got the shutter speed 1/100 and aperture 2.8 values reversed in your data field).  The reddish color is also a bit disconcerting, especially when taken with regard to the colorless surrounding.  If that is the natural color, not a lot to do with it, but if not, maybe desaturating it, or toning it down a bit to the more natural colors. Finally, the size is a bit small, and while it is not a huge issue, being slightly larger would have helped, I think, because people could have seen some of the details better.  There is a tutorial under Learn>Tutorials called (something to the effect of) resizing for dpc.  If you have not looked at it, and if you are continuing to have to use smaller dimensions to get under 150, it might be worth reading.   If you have any questions, comments, or if I need to further clarify anything, please let me know. karmat', '2007-01-18 00:15:30.000000', NULL, 0),
