(3258285, 448901, 15711, '<td valign="top">I''ve come back to this several times without commenting.  It finally hit me - this looks like Frank Langella. Has anyone ever told you that?<br/> <br/>I like the duotone treatment. It suits the meditative, simple emotion of this shot.</td>', 'I''ve come back to this several times without commenting.  It finally hit me - this looks like Frank Langella. Has anyone ever told you that? I like the duotone treatment. It suits the meditative, simple emotion of this shot.', '2007-01-19 23:53:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(3258543, 449178, 9581, '<td valign="top">Wow that looks like a potential Barrett-Jackson''s truck. Congratulations on second or red ribbon.. This turned out really good with the colors, I like the way the truck stands out from everything else.</td>', 'Wow that looks like a potential Barrett-Jackson''s truck. Congratulations on second or red ribbon.. This turned out really good with the colors, I like the way the truck stands out from everything else.', '2007-01-20 01:41:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(3259098, 447100, 46819, '<td valign="top"><b>(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯) </b> <br/> <br/>Well, I''ve been looking over and critiquing some of the other entries for the procrastination challenge recently and this is, for the most part, one of the more interesting. I think that you really captured an emotion here, it really makes the viewer of the photograph think, and as ''mpeters'' wrote in your comments, the absence of the children is very powerful, if not more so than with them in the shot.<br/> <br/>Your execution of the shot looks technically sound, except for the slightly low contrast settings. According to your processes, you toyed with the hue and sat, the tones, and other levels but not the brightness and contrast so much. Just a slightly higher level would have brought out some of the shadows and details bringing your image off of the page and I can say with confidence that doing so would easily land you in the top third of the competition. <br/> <br/>The cloud cover works very well, and gives the image a flat lighting that just emphasises the lack of action in the subject (i.e. no children) perfectly. Your choice of black and white added to the shot well, although I feel that perhaps a little colour would have brought it out. If you check the tutorials, there''s one on b&amp;w, and it explains how by just adding the tiniest amounts of colour, the image can still look as though on a greyscale, but with emphasis on some of the subjects and a greater depth of field. <br/> <br/>Otherwise, despite a slight digression off of the subject of the competition, slightly more post-editing would secure this into the high 5''s if not 6''s. <br/> <br/>Good luck with all future competitions!<br/> <br/><b>.´¯`-&gt;Lucien&lt;-´¯`.</b> <br/></td>', '(¯`._.[Critique Club]._.´¯)   Well, I''ve been looking over and critiquing some of the other entries for the procrastination challenge recently and this is, for the most part, one of the more interesting. I think that you really captured an emotion here, it really makes the viewer of the photograph think, and as ''mpeters'' wrote in your comments, the absence of the children is very powerful, if not more so than with them in the shot. Your execution of the shot looks technically sound, except for the slightly low contrast settings. According to your processes, you toyed with the hue and sat, the tones, and other levels but not the brightness and contrast so much. Just a slightly higher level would have brought out some of the shadows and details bringing your image off of the page and I can say with confidence that doing so would easily land you in the top third of the competition.  The cloud cover works very well, and gives the image a flat lighting that just emphasises the lack of action in the subject (i.e. no children) perfectly. Your choice of black and white added to the shot well, although I feel that perhaps a little colour would have brought it out. If you check the tutorials, there''s one on b&w, and it explains how by just adding the tiniest amounts of colour, the image can still look as though on a greyscale, but with emphasis on some of the subjects and a greater depth of field.  Otherwise, despite a slight digression off of the subject of the competition, slightly more post-editing would secure this into the high 5''s if not 6''s.  Good luck with all future competitions! .´¯`->Lucien<-´¯`. ', '2007-01-20 09:46:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(3260283, 448851, 70124, '<td valign="top">Best of top 3.</td>', 'Best of top 3.', '2007-01-20 18:23:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(3264033, 448077, 16197, '<td valign="top"><b>From the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>How are you doing today?  I am going to touch on a few items with your image.<br/> <br/><b>Colour</b>;<br/>Your colour looks fairly nice here, I just think there is a bit to much yellow on your chest, which could be from your spotlight?  everything else seems to balance nicely, the blue/gray of the shorts matches the weights, your skin tone matches the carpet, bottle lable matches wieghts.  Very nice job.<br/> <br/><b>DOF/Focus</b>;<br/>Focus looks really great here, everything apears to be in focus. I can even make out the carpet strands, and read the beer label.<br/> <br/><b>Post</b>;<br/>You didn''t do much for post, and mostly it appears you didn''t have to.  The blacks look black the lights are light, and the medium tones are nice.  One thing I might have done, is maybe dodge a bit on the chair, to see more of the leg coming down, and a hint of seat.  Just a horizon''t glint you might say.  The way it appears now, at first glance you seem to be floating.<br/> <br/><b>Off Items</b>;<br/>As I said before, your chest seems a bit yellow.  Also, the back of your head fades a tad much I think into the background.  A really soft highlight to see the back of your head may give a stronger feel. You could do this with a reflector. <br/> <br/><b>Other</b>;<br/>I think this is a clever idea.  You captured the procrastination very well.  I just noticed the beer cap still on the bottle?  I am assuming it was left to see the top of the bottle., if that is the case you might have angled the light a bit different to get the top.  But if that is not the case and a highlight.. than you are good to go.<br/> <br/>Nice Job<br/> <br/>Andrew ''littlegett''</td>', 'From the Critique Club  How are you doing today?  I am going to touch on a few items with your image. Colour;Your colour looks fairly nice here, I just think there is a bit to much yellow on your chest, which could be from your spotlight?  everything else seems to balance nicely, the blue/gray of the shorts matches the weights, your skin tone matches the carpet, bottle lable matches wieghts.  Very nice job. DOF/Focus;Focus looks really great here, everything apears to be in focus. I can even make out the carpet strands, and read the beer label. Post;You didn''t do much for post, and mostly it appears you didn''t have to.  The blacks look black the lights are light, and the medium tones are nice.  One thing I might have done, is maybe dodge a bit on the chair, to see more of the leg coming down, and a hint of seat.  Just a horizon''t glint you might say.  The way it appears now, at first glance you seem to be floating. Off Items;As I said before, your chest seems a bit yellow.  Also, the back of your head fades a tad much I think into the background.  A really soft highlight to see the back of your head may give a stronger feel. You could do this with a reflector.  Other;I think this is a clever idea.  You captured the procrastination very well.  I just noticed the beer cap still on the bottle?  I am assuming it was left to see the top of the bottle., if that is the case you might have angled the light a bit different to get the top.  But if that is not the case and a highlight.. than you are good to go. Nice Job Andrew ''littlegett''', '2007-01-21 22:39:28.000000', NULL, 0),
